\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
We need to remember that different biomes are not suppose to be better or worse. Ideally, each biome takes the crystal the same amount of time to pass through but forces it to pass through in a different way. This means that the average height of all the biomes needs to be roughly the same.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
The best way to a}{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
c}{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
hi}{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
e}{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
ve this is going to be by making the average height of each biome the same. We can do this by imagining that each biome is a cup and is given a bit of playdough to fill it with. The biome can shape the dough in any way to fill the cup, IE: multiple long sticks, one large or several small pebbles, a hollow dome, but no matter what its the same volume of dough and thus the same volume of crystal can fill the remaining space.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
This is especially important with things like mountains were the idea is that there are a lot of high points. To balance that out there needs to be an equal amount of low points.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
This will mean that if we view the entire map all at once it will look strangely flat, but the player will never be able to do that save for maybe the topograhical map.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
This is good.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
A flatter map means that we need to add less mechanics that would otherwise make the crystal harder for the player to control. It also means that the crystal has a higher chance of actually branching through the entire map rather than being naturally funneled somewhere.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
The true randomness of height will come from features, like canyons and lakes.}
